            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Hi!\r\n\r\n> When using `saiw\"` to add double-quotes, the cursor is moved to the start of the word, as show below:\r\n\r\nFor reference: this is intended. #1409 contains links to more details.\r\n\r\n> I would prefer that the cursor stays at the same position, regardless of the motion. I don't understand how to approach this problem - should I use markers, should I save the cursor position before `Minisurround.add()`, or...?\r\n\r\nMy honest suggestion would be to just get used to it, because this is consistent with the built-in behavior (like with `yiw`, `guiw` or any built-in operator). If the latter will change in the future, so will `sa` operator.\r\n\r\nAs to pointers... All of them will be hacky and potentially not reliable in the long term and/or quite convoluted (because Vim's operator implementation is). You can indeed try to copy the 'mini.surround' approach:\r\n- [This](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/61a5d44a8f31370710e6a998c5a8ee7e61c7e576/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1199) creates expression mapping.\r\n- [This](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/61a5d44a8f31370710e6a998c5a8ee7e61c7e576/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1266-L1283) creates function right hand side. Here instead of `vim.o.operatorfunc = 'v:lua.MiniSurround.add'` use something like `v:lua.my_surround_add` which caches cursor position, calls `MiniSurround.add()`, and restores cached cursor position.\r\n\r\nHope this helps.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Hi!</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">When using <code class=\"notranslate\">saiw\"</code> to add double-quotes, the cursor is moved to the start of the word, as show below:</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">For reference: this is intended. <a class=\"issue-link js-issue-link\" data-error-text=\"Failed to load title\" data-id=\"2750004534\" data-permission-text=\"Title is private\" data-url=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1409\" data-hovercard-type=\"issue\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1409/hovercard\" href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1409\">#1409</a> contains links to more details.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I would prefer that the cursor stays at the same position, regardless of the motion. I don't understand how to approach this problem - should I use markers, should I save the cursor position before <code class=\"notranslate\">Minisurround.add()</code>, or...?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">My honest suggestion would be to just get used to it, because this is consistent with the built-in behavior (like with <code class=\"notranslate\">yiw</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">guiw</code> or any built-in operator). If the latter will change in the future, so will <code class=\"notranslate\">sa</code> operator.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">As to pointers... All of them will be hacky and potentially not reliable in the long term and/or quite convoluted (because Vim's operator implementation is). You can indeed try to copy the 'mini.surround' approach:</p>\n<ul dir=\"auto\">\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/61a5d44a8f31370710e6a998c5a8ee7e61c7e576/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1199\">This</a> creates expression mapping.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/61a5d44a8f31370710e6a998c5a8ee7e61c7e576/lua/mini/surround.lua#L1266-L1283\">This</a> creates function right hand side. Here instead of <code class=\"notranslate\">vim.o.operatorfunc = 'v:lua.MiniSurround.add'</code> use something like <code class=\"notranslate\">v:lua.my_surround_add</code> which caches cursor position, calls <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniSurround.add()</code>, and restores cached cursor position.</li>\n</ul>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Hope this helps.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Hi!\n\nWhen using saiw\" to add double-quotes, the cursor is moved to the start of the word, as show below:\n\nFor reference: this is intended. #1409 contains links to more details.\n\nI would prefer that the cursor stays at the same position, regardless of the motion. I don't understand how to approach this problem - should I use markers, should I save the cursor position before Minisurround.add(), or...?\n\nMy honest suggestion would be to just get used to it, because this is consistent with the built-in behavior (like with yiw, guiw or any built-in operator). If the latter will change in the future, so will sa operator.\nAs to pointers... All of them will be hacky and potentially not reliable in the long term and/or quite convoluted (because Vim's operator implementation is). You can indeed try to copy the 'mini.surround' approach:\n\nThis creates expression mapping.\nThis creates function right hand side. Here instead of vim.o.operatorfunc = 'v:lua.MiniSurround.add' use something like v:lua.my_surround_add which caches cursor position, calls MiniSurround.add(), and restores cached cursor position.\n\nHope this helps.",
